{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-2:283\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Both passages condition an alternative on travel and failure to find an ordinary resource: 2:283 names a pledge when no scribe is found, while 5:6 directs tayammum when water is not found. This places the purification concession within a wider pattern of adapting a rule to constrained circumstances.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:283\",\"ar\":\"وَإِن كُنتُمْ عَلَىٰ سَفَرٍۢ وَلَمْ تَجِدُوا۟ كَاتِبًۭا فَرِهَٰنٌۭ مَّقْبُوضَةٌۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَإِن كُنتُم مَّرْضَىٰٓ أَوْ عَلَىٰ سَفَرٍ أَوْ جَآءَ أَحَدٌۭ مِّنكُم مِّنَ ٱلْغَآئِطِ أَوْ لَٰمَسْتُمُ ٱلنِّسَآءَ فَلَمْ تَجِدُوا۟ مَآءًۭ فَتَيَمَّمُوا۟ صَعِيدًۭا طَيِّبًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared sequence عَلَىٰ سَفَرٍۢ وَلَمْ تَجِدُوا۟ leads in both verses to an alternative arrangement.\",\"limits\":\"The pledge is not a substitute for purification, and 2:283 does not state that its arrangement is meant to relieve hardship.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:286\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s denial of intended ḥaraj parallels 2:286’s limit of responsibility to capacity and plea against an unbearable load; 5:6 applies that concern to a concrete ritual concession.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:286\",\"ar\":\"لَا يُكَلِّفُ ٱللَّهُ نَفْسًا إِلَّا وُسْعَهَا\"},{\"ref\":\"2:286\",\"ar\":\"رَبَّنَا وَلَا تُحَمِّلْنَا مَا لَا طَاقَةَ لَنَا بِهِۦ\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"مَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيَجْعَلَ عَلَيْكُم مِّنْ حَرَجٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names hardship directly and follows its denial with an alternative procedure when water is unavailable.\",\"limits\":\"The passages use different terms and settings; 2:286 does not specify ritual purification.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:15\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s يُطَهِّرَكُمْ and 3:15’s مُطَهَّرَةٌ share the ط-ه-ر root in distinct forms: one names a divine aim in a ritual directive, the other a promised state of spouses in the Garden.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلَٰكِن يُرِيدُ لِيُطَهِّرَكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"3:15\",\"ar\":\"وَأَزْوَٰجٌۭ مُّطَهَّرَةٌۭ وَرِضْوَٰنٌۭ مِّنَ ٱللَّهِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The repeated purification root links the focus’s closing purpose clause with the afterlife promise in 3:15.\",\"limits\":\"The parallel does not establish that the ritual produces or anticipates the state described in 3:15.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:42\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Both verses make God the agent of purification: 3:42 places طَهَّرَكِ between two statements of God choosing Mary, while 5:6 names purification as God’s aim for the addressed community.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"3:42\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ ٱصْطَفَىٰكِ وَطَهَّرَكِ وَٱصْطَفَىٰكِ عَلَىٰ نِسَآءِ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلَٰكِن يُرِيدُ لِيُطَهِّرَكُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared ط-ه-ر verb and explicit divine agency connect the two passages.\",\"limits\":\"Mary’s purification is not described as this ritual, and the verses address different recipients and circumstances.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:45\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:55\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s ليطهركم and 3:55’s ومطهرك من الذين كفروا share the purification root but apply it to different settings: ritual practice here and Jesus’s separation from disbelievers there.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلَٰكِن يُرِيدُ لِيُطَهِّرَكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"3:55\",\"ar\":\"وَمُطَهِّرُكَ مِنَ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟\"}],\"activation\":\"The divine agent and the shared ط-ه-ر root make the difference in the objects of purification visible.\",\"limits\":\"The parallel does not establish a shared mechanism or make the focus’s purification social separation.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:72\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The focus names bodily faces with وُجُوهَكُمْ, while 3:72 uses وَجْهَ ٱلنَّهَارِ. A possible link through “face” as a front or outward aspect could put facial washing beside a public display of belief, but that link is not established by these verses alone.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"فَٱغْسِلُوا۟ وُجُوهَكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"3:72\",\"ar\":\"وَجْهَ ٱلنَّهَارِ\"}],\"missing\":\"Lexical evidence for the sense of وَجْهَ ٱلنَّهَارِ and a contextual reason to connect that public strategy with the focus’s ritual washing could establish whether the shared word adds anything beyond root resemblance.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:103\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The completion of نعمة in 5:6 can be heard alongside 3:103’s concrete account of divine favor: God turns enemies into brothers by reconciling their hearts. The shared نعمة الله عليكم wording gives the focus’s ritual provision a place in a wider account of communal benefit.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"3:103\",\"ar\":\"وَٱذْكُرُوا۟ نِعْمَتَ ٱللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ إِذْ كُنتُمْ أَعْدَآءًۭ فَأَلَّفَ بَيْنَ قُلُوبِكُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both verses pair نعمة with عَلَيْكُمْ; 5:6 also makes gratitude the hoped-for response.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not specify social reconciliation as the content of this favor; 3:103 names that verse’s particular favor.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:111\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:123\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus and 3:123 both end a statement of divine benefit with لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ. In 3:123 the occasion is victory at Badr; the repeated ending frames gratitude as a response to divine provision across distinct circumstances.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"3:123\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَدْ نَصَرَكُمُ ٱللَّهُ بِبَدْرٍۢ وَأَنتُمْ أَذِلَّةٌۭ ۖ فَٱتَّقُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names completion of favor immediately before the same gratitude formula that follows aid at Badr.\",\"limits\":\"The shared ending does not make ritual purity and military aid equivalent benefits.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:153\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"After battle grief, 3:154 describes God’s purposes as testing and يَمَحِّصَ what is in hearts; 5:6 pairs the denial of ḥaraj with God’s purpose to يُطَهِّرَكُمْ. The passages may contrast inward refinement through ordeal with purification through an accommodated rite.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"3:154\",\"ar\":\"وَلِيَبْتَلِىَ ٱللَّهُ مَا فِى صُدُورِكُمْ وَلِيُمَحِّصَ مَا فِى قُلُوبِكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"مَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيَجْعَلَ عَلَيْكُم مِّنْ حَرَجٍۢ وَلَٰكِن يُرِيدُ لِيُطَهِّرَكُمْ\"}],\"missing\":\"Lexical evidence establishing how يَمَحِّصَ relates to يُطَهِّرَكُمْ, and further context linking the battle ordeal with ritual practice, could decide whether this is a meaningful contrast in modes of purification.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:156\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:177\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:193\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:200\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:4\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The earth for tayammum is صَعِيدًا طَيِّبًا, while 4:4 uses the related طِبْنَ for women’s willing consent. The shared ط-ي-ب root touches on suitability in two different legal settings.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"فَتَيَمَّمُوا۟ صَعِيدًۭا طَيِّبًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"4:4\",\"ar\":\"فَإِن طِبْنَ لَكُمْ عَن شَىْءٍۢ مِّنْهُ نَفْسًۭا فَكُلُوهُ هَنِيٓـًۭٔا مَّرِيٓـًۭٔا\"}],\"activation\":\"The same root qualifies both the selected earth and the women’s consent in 4:4.\",\"limits\":\"The verses do not equate clean earth with consent or connect the two rulings beyond the root and a broad suitability sense.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:7\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:20\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:26\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"4:26 states that God’s will is to clarify, guide, and turn toward the addressees; 5:6 likewise repeats يُرِيدُ اللَّهُ with purpose lām clauses. This places the purification rule within a broader presentation of divine guidance through legal instruction.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:26\",\"ar\":\"يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيُبَيِّنَ لَكُمْ وَيَهْدِيَكُمْ سُنَنَ ٱلَّذِينَ مِن قَبْلِكُمْ وَيَتُوبَ عَلَيْكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"مَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيَجْعَلَ عَلَيْكُم مِّنْ حَرَجٍۢ وَلَٰكِن يُرِيدُ لِيُطَهِّرَكُمْ وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages make divine will explicit and state purposes through lām clauses in the context of legal rulings.\",\"limits\":\"The shared construction does not show that the specific rulings have the same purpose; 4:26 names guidance and repentance, while 5:6 names purification, favor, and gratitude.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:27\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"4:27 sets God’s will that believers turn back to Him against the will of desire-followers that they swerve greatly. The focus’s stated will to purify and complete favor can be heard as one expression of the divine aim opposed to that swerve.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:27\",\"ar\":\"وَٱللَّهُ يُرِيدُ أَن يَتُوبَ عَلَيْكُمْ وَيُرِيدُ ٱلَّذِينَ يَتَّبِعُونَ ٱلشَّهَوَٰتِ أَن تَمِيلُوا۟ مَيْلًا عَظِيمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلَٰكِن يُرِيدُ لِيُطَهِّرَكُمْ وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both verses repeat يُرِيدُ and address the same plural community; 4:27 explicitly opposes divine purpose to an alternative human aim.\",\"limits\":\"4:27 does not identify the ritual in 5:6 as a response to desire-followers or define purification as repentance.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:28\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"4:28 explicitly says God wills to lighten the burden on the addressees; 5:6 denies that He intends ḥaraj and supplies tayammum when water is unavailable. Together they make relief an expressed principle of the focus’s ritual ruling.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:28\",\"ar\":\"يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ أَن يُخَفِّفَ عَنكُمْ ۚ وَخُلِقَ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ ضَعِيفًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"مَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيَجْعَلَ عَلَيْكُم مِّنْ حَرَجٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The verses share God as subject, the verb يُرِيدُ, and direct reference to burden or lightening for the addressees.\",\"limits\":\"4:28 does not name purification or identify its subject with tayammum; the connection rests on the shared stated concern for easing.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:43\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"4:43 repeats the focus’s illness, travel, event, water-absence, and tayammum sequence almost word for word, and explicitly places it after a warning about approaching prayer in certain states. It supplies a direct parallel that situates the focus’s procedure within prayer access.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:43\",\"ar\":\"وَإِن كُنتُم مَّرْضَىٰٓ أَوْ عَلَىٰ سَفَرٍ أَوْ جَآءَ أَحَدٌۭ مِّنكُم مِّنَ ٱلْغَآئِطِ أَوْ لَٰمَسْتُمُ ٱلنِّسَآءَ فَلَمْ تَجِدُوا۟ مَآءًۭ فَتَيَمَّمُوا۟ صَعِيدًۭا طَيِّبًۭا فَٱمْسَحُوا۟ بِوُجُوهِكُمْ وَأَيْدِيكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَإِن كُنتُم مَّرْضَىٰٓ أَوْ عَلَىٰ سَفَرٍ أَوْ جَآءَ أَحَدٌۭ مِّنكُم مِّنَ ٱلْغَآئِطِ أَوْ لَٰمَسْتُمُ ٱلنِّسَآءَ فَلَمْ تَجِدُوا۟ مَآءًۭ فَتَيَمَّمُوا۟ صَعِيدًۭا طَيِّبًۭا فَٱمْسَحُوا۟ بِوُجُوهِكُمْ وَأَيْدِيكُم\"}],\"activation\":\"The repeated conditional sequence and the preceding الصلاة clause in 4:43 connect the alternative purification directly to prayer.\",\"limits\":\"4:43 does not provide the focus’s initial washing and wiping instructions and does not include its مِنْهُ phrase or its stated purpose of completing favor.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:46\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:49\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"4:49 rejects people who يزكون أنفسهم and assigns purification to God; 5:6 likewise makes God the subject of the will to يُطَهِّرَكُمْ. The parallel emphasizes divine agency, though the verbs and concerns differ.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:49\",\"ar\":\"أَلَمْ تَرَ إِلَى ٱلَّذِينَ يُزَكُّونَ أَنفُسَهُم ۚ بَلِ ٱللَّهُ يُزَكِّى مَن يَشَآءُ\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلَٰكِن يُرِيدُ لِيُطَهِّرَكُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"God is the explicit agent of purification in both passages, and the focus presents purification as His purpose rather than the addressees’ self-claim.\",\"limits\":\"The focus uses ط-ه-ر for ritual purification, while 4:49 uses ز-ك-و to challenge self-ascribed purity; the verses do not equate those senses.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:57\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"4:57’s أَزْوَاجٌ مُّطَهَّرَةٌ shares its purification root with the focus’s ليطهركم, but describes a promised state in the Garden. In 4:57 this promise follows the contrast between punishment and the believers’ lasting shade.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:57\",\"ar\":\"لَّهُمْ فِيهَآ أَزْوَٰجٌۭ مُّطَهَّرَةٌۭ ۖ وَنُدْخِلُهُمْ ظِلًّۭا ظَلِيلًا\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلَٰكِن يُرِيدُ لِيُطَهِّرَكُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared ط-ه-ر root invites a comparison between present ritual purification and the future state described in 4:57.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not say that ritual purification causes or guarantees the promised state.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:82\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:90\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Both verses use a negated جعل construction to mark a limit on what may be imposed: 5:6 denies hardship upon the addressees, while 4:90 denies a way against those who withdraw and offer peace.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"مَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيَجْعَلَ عَلَيْكُم مِّنْ حَرَجٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:90\",\"ar\":\"فَإِنِ ٱعْتَزَلُوكُمْ فَلَمْ يُقَٰتِلُوكُمْ وَأَلْقَوْا۟ إِلَيْكُمُ ٱلسَّلَمَ فَمَا جَعَلَ ٱللَّهُ لَكُمْ عَلَيْهِمْ سَبِيلًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The repeated negated جعل language and the legal boundary in each verse make the construction parallel.\",\"limits\":\"One passage concerns ritual hardship and the other conflict; the parallel does not make their rules interchangeable.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:99\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The sequence around 4:99 describes people unable to find a way and says God may pardon them; 5:6 accommodates illness, travel, or lack of water and denies intended hardship. Together they present divine allowance as responsive to human constraint across different settings.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:98\",\"ar\":\"لَا يَسْتَطِيعُونَ حِيلَةًۭ وَلَا يَهْتَدُونَ سَبِيلًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"4:99\",\"ar\":\"فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ عَسَى ٱللَّهُ أَن يَعْفُوَ عَنْهُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"مَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيَجْعَلَ عَلَيْكُم مِّنْ حَرَجٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The surrounding incapacity and pardon in 4:98–99 resonate with the focus’s conditional concessions and denial of hardship.\",\"limits\":\"4:99 is not a ritual rule, and pardon there is not explicitly identified with the focus’s idea of ḥaraj.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
{"type":"turn.completed","usage":{"input_tokens":52186,"cached_input_tokens":13056,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":16780,"reasoning_output_tokens":11899}}
